!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/37/ltr8/http/problem-1.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/37/m/meta.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/37/m/core.tn"
@doc:"""
  The error body this project writes when a request cannot be fulfilled.

  This is tson-http's own schema, maintained here. It began as a copy of tson-cli's diagnostics.tn
  and has since diverged, which is the right outcome rather than drift: a CLI reports on files and a
  server reports on requests, and holding one shape across both would have meant neither fitting.

  problem follows RFC 9457 (Problem Details for HTTP APIs, obsoleting 7807) so that ordinary HTTP
  tooling recognises the body: type, title, status, detail and instance are its five members, and
  errors is an extension member of exactly the shape §3.1 of that RFC uses for its own
  multiple-validation-errors example.

  diagnostic stays close to what a TSON read produces, because that is what it reports. It is not
  RFC 9457's per-error shape -- that RFC suggests detail and pointer, which would throw away the
  code vocabulary, the two-ended location model and the source positions §8.1 requires.

  Versioning, per §3.5: a shape change to a PUBLISHED schema is a new name, never an edit, because a
  document that named the old one must go on resolving. Nothing here is published yet, so this file
  is edited in place and stays -1. When that changes, adding so much as an enum member below becomes
  problem-2.tn, and this document keeps being served alongside it.
  """
{
  @doc:"""
    A hand-written copy of io.ltr8.tson.compiler.Diagnostic.Code, which is the source of truth;
    TsonProblemSchemaTest is the only thing that checks the two agree.

    Eleven members are not a verdict on the request, and they differ by who could not give one.
    NOT_IMPLEMENTED: this library could not check a construct the body uses -- the same split the CLI rides
    its exit codes on (70, not 1) -- a 501. BIND_MISMATCH: a schema and the Java class bound to it disagree,
    or the class refused a value the schema admits, which is this server's own wiring; its message names a
    server class, so it is a 500 and the boundary drops it from the body entirely. The five SCHEMA_* codes:
    no schema was obtained, one code per reason. The remaining four, with SCHEMA_NOT_PERMITTED and
    SCHEMA_TOO_LARGE, are refusals: this processor declined under its own policy and limits, and the next one
    along may accept the same body in full.

    Not being a verdict does not settle the status, and the SCHEMA_* five are where that shows.
    SCHEMA_UNREACHABLE is a 502 and SCHEMA_TIMEOUT a 504 -- the reference was fine and the world was
    not. SCHEMA_NOT_PERMITTED (a reference this deployment will not fetch), SCHEMA_NOT_FOUND (one
    nothing serves) and SCHEMA_TOO_LARGE (one whose document exceeds the bytes a schema may be) are the
    document's own doing and are 400s, because the sender is who can name something else. Nothing was
    checked in any of the five; only two of them are worth retrying, and those two answer only when nothing
    else in the body is the sender's to fix, since a retry would otherwise reach the same answer.

    They are declared here even though a client should never see the first two, because this enum
    copies Diagnostic.Code rather than the subset one server happens to emit.

    CONFUSABLE_NAMES, RESTRICTED_CHARACTER and RESTRICTED_SCRIPT are [TSON-DATA] §8.2's name hygiene,
    one code per rule, and LIMIT_EXCEEDED is §9.1's nesting bound. They are this deployment's CONFIGURATION
    speaking -- ProcessorConfig decides which scripts a name or token may carry and how deep a body may
    nest -- so a refusal says nothing about whether the body is valid. It is still the client's to fix, by a
    rename, a character, or a shallower body, so the three name rules are 400s and the limit a 413.

    The rule rides the code rather than a field beside it, which is the right shape: a consumer routes
    on the code, and the three want three different fixes -- rename one of a colliding pair, change a
    character outside the identifier profile, or relax the level. A second enum would only restate what
    the code already fixes and would be free to disagree with it.

    SCOPE_NOT_ADMITTED is a body opening a schema scope (!!schema) at a position whose type is not a
    scoped one, so the schema gives it nowhere to open -- [TSON-SCHEMA] §7.8's typed-position
    restriction, a resolver error and a 400. A push at a scoped position that does not admit foreign
    schemas is the cell rule's refusal instead, a VALIDATION_ERROR.

    An atom refuses a token in two categories, so it carries two codes. §5.2 splits the refusal -- a
    token the atom's grammar rejects is a parse error, a parsed value violating the atom's range is a
    validation error -- and §8.1 files the halves apart, the first as a resolver error and the second as
    a validation one. ATOM_FORM_INVALID is the first, ATOM_CONSTRAINT_VIOLATION the second. Both are
    verdicts on the document and so both are 400; they are two codes because the fixes differ -- rewrite
    the token, or change the value it denotes.
    """
  diagnostic_code => !enum [
    FIELD_REQUIRED FIELD_FIXED FIELD_GROUP TYPE_MISMATCH WRONG_ARITY UNKNOWN_TYPE_REF SCOPE_NOT_ADMITTED
    ATOM_FORM_INVALID ATOM_CONSTRAINT_VIOLATION UNRECOGNIZED_FIELD DUPLICATE_MAP_KEY DUPLICATE_FIELD CONFUSABLE_NAMES
    RESTRICTED_CHARACTER RESTRICTED_SCRIPT SCHEMA_ERROR UNKNOWN_TYPE VALIDATION_ERROR NOT_IMPLEMENTED
    BIND_MISMATCH
    LIMIT_EXCEEDED SCHEMA_NOT_PERMITTED SCHEMA_NOT_FOUND SCHEMA_UNREACHABLE SCHEMA_TIMEOUT SCHEMA_TOO_LARGE
  ]

  @doc:"""
    One problem, located at up to two ends: the value in the data (path, data_position) and the rule
    in the schema (schema_id, schema_pointer, schema_position). Either end may be absent -- a problem
    found in a schema has no data, and a schemaless read has no schema.

    A position is "line:column:byte_offset", all three 1-based except byte_offset, which counts
    UTF-8 bytes from 0 ([TSON-DATA] §8.1). The offsets are into the request body as sent.

    Every member here is a location or the problem itself; nothing is a payload meaningful to one code.
    Why a schema could not be obtained is the code, one per reason, so a body cannot state a code and a
    reason that contradict each other.

    LIMIT_EXCEEDED is the same shape one layer out: a §9.1 bound this deployment reads within, so
    `expected` and `actual` carry the depth admitted against the depth reached and nothing states the
    policy, which is published once at /.well-known/tson-deployment.

    A refusal under a §8.2 policy (CONFUSABLE_NAMES, RESTRICTED_CHARACTER, RESTRICTED_SCRIPT) carries
    nothing beyond its code. The level and the Unicode data version it was judged under are properties
    of the processor, not of the problem -- constant for the life of the process -- so they are stated
    once, in the deployment's published acceptance profile, rather than on every refusal, where they
    would arrive only after the fact. Which rule fired is the code, one per rule.
    """
  diagnostic => {
    path?: text
    schema_pointer?: text
    schema_id?: text
    code: diagnostic_code
    message: text
    expected?: text
    actual?: text
    data_position?: text
    schema_position?: text
  }
  @doc:"""
    RFC 9457's five members, plus errors.

    type identifies the class of failure and is the member to match on: it is stable where title is
    prose, and it dereferences. Absent means RFC 9457's "about:blank" -- no semantics beyond the
    status code. title summarises the type; detail describes this one occurrence. instance
    identifies this occurrence. Both type and instance are URI references, so either may be relative, per
    that RFC (§3.1.1, §3.1.5) -- which is why they are uri_reference and not uri, which requires a scheme.

    status is restated here so a problem that is stored, logged or forwarded stays self-describing
    once separated from its response.

    errors is empty for a failure that produced no diagnostic at all -- an unsupported media type, an
    unacceptable Accept -- which is why it is a list rather than a required single value.
    """
  problem => {
    type?: uri_reference
    title: text
    status: int32
    detail?: text
    instance?: uri_reference
    errors: [diagnostic]
  }
}
